A||||12||DXR_DAD||Junk Yard Tools??||||10||
Z||000000||DXR_DAD||02-01-2001||10:11 PM||||Does anyone have a toolbox just for your junk yard trips? If not, do you have a list of what you should have when you go. Fiero parts removal only  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>DXR_DAD||||reg||10
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||02-01-2001||10:31 PM||||I have a little kit I use...<P><BR>A Torx Bit..(Dash panels,Marker lights....ect)<BR>7mm socket & ratchet<BR>Philips<BR>Flathead<BR>Ajustable Wrench<P>And a small flathead to pop off the door tabs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif"> <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!!!!!!!!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!!!"</B>||||reg||6
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||02-01-2001||10:46 PM||||don't forget the visegrips!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||wilburt||02-01-2001||11:26 PM||||I always carry a five gallon bucket with all the tools I think I'll need, plus others, and always forget one tool or another. With the bucket, I can see everything, carry parts, and I don't have to bend down as far to pick it up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-01-2001||11:29 PM||||I usually bring along a 10mm and 13mm sockets and wrenches as well as all the other items mentioned by Haze.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||02-01-2001||11:36 PM||||Can't remember that seat belt torx size??<BR>I have that torx socket in the bottom of my box, just can't remember the size.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||02-01-2001||11:41 PM||||Seat belts and original calipers T-50 torx.||||reg||1
Z||000007||jch82||02-02-2001||12:05 AM||||definitely visegrips! they always come in handy<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Before you criticize someone, walk a mile in his shoes. That way, if he  gets angry, he'll be a mile way - and barefoot....||||reg||12
Z||000008||1FieroGTRacingInc||02-02-2001||01:15 AM||||all that mentioned above .... plus the wire cutter's..   alway's handy for me...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||02-02-2001||01:29 AM||||You can almost completely dissasemble a Fiero with 7mm, 10mm, 13mm, 15mm wrenches.  If you are getting suspension parts you will need the 16mm, 17mm and 16mm wrenches as well.<BR>For the interior stuff defeinitely the torx bits and 7mm wrench both medium and small phillips and flat screw drivers.  Vicegrips or robogrips are a must and take along some diagonal linemans pliers for cutting brake/clutch lines if want the master, slave or calipers quickly.<P>I have a small belt pack that holds everything I need less my 3/8 drive socket set.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||02-03-2001||04:28 PM||||I do lots of scavenging. I start with a small plastic toolbox, so it wont rust. Yards always seem to be covered in small lakes. waterproof boots. one of those screwdrivers that bend in middle with a small set of torx, phillips,straight tips. and and adapter for sockets from 7mm-14mm. side cutters/dykes and some reg screwdrivers to use as prybars. a few comb wrenches from 7mm - 14 too. definately a couple visegrips. small claw hammer. waterpump pliers. If its suspension or major engine stuff, i let them get it for me, they got a wrecker to lift it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||02-03-2001||04:37 PM||||Ditto most of the above, plus end nippers and a chisel for rivets, and a lug wrench as needed.  Also vinyl gloves, paper towels, baggies, and newspapers.  Fortunately, the local self-service yards provide wheelbarrows.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||02-04-2001||01:22 PM||||I use the bucket method. what goes in the bucket depends partly what I'm after. I always take my 3/8's metric set and my Skew set. <P>Skew is on of those multipurpose screwdriver sets with a bunch of diferant size bits. Mine's had a couple things added. like a T30 bit. Main thing is the angled gear head peice. That gear head is about a 100 degree angle. It will make things like take out speaker screws on the dash really easy. The gear head gives much more control than the POS fliexible shaft things. It's made by a company called Spec Tools.<P>I take the 1/2 set maybe if I'm doing big items or wheel work. I usually take cutters. ||||reg||1
